  "notes": "Texas-neotraditional in the mainstream — steel-and-fiddle forward, big-ballad-driven, and the entry's own words, \"deliberately anti-programming.\" That negative is authored as a positive absence: no clap layer, no programmed perc, no `bass808` block, and \"no programmed sub anywhere\" is quoted from the entry's low-end line. The drums are \"cross-stick verses opening to a full backbeat, kick on 1 and 3, brushes on the slowest cuts,\" and the documented band is 75–90 on the ballad-dominant cuts, so this is the narrowest and slowest tempo window of the 2020s cohort here. Harmony is \"I–IV–V, I–V–vi–IV, with V7 honky-tonk colour\" — the dominant colour in `extensions`, the degrees in the families. The counter carries the identity because the entry says the instruments do: \"pedal steel and fiddle as co-lead voices.\" Melody is \"full-throat baritone-tenor with a wide chorus range, a pronounced country break, and long sustained notes at peaks.\" The arrangement is described as \"a deliberately pre-2010 form\" — \"no post-chorus hooks, no drop-outs\" — which is the near-zero drop-out probability. ⚠ Boundary flag: the Texas dancehall uptempo material is two-beat/shuffle territory and belongs to `country-shuffle` and `country-train`. ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: \"palette and lane well established; tempo evidence thin [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
